[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299856 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75dc16f3-b24c-59e8-aac9-c1ea4729e37b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298066}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75dc16f3-b24c-59e8-aac9-c1ea4729e37b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298066} :answer-field/id #uuid "70277a6d-54df-5d86-932e-e42ed6a7a735" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6597267d-a0da-5d5e-b28e-9dd4c1c2688b" :question/math-academy-id "q-320553" :question/problem "Given that the area spanned between the vectors $\\text{u}=⟨-\\frac{4}{3},7⟩$ and $\\text{v}=⟨a,-9⟩$ equals zero, what is the value of $a?$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{3} & a \\\\ 7 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{4}{3} & a \\\\ 7 & -9\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{4}{3})\\cdot (-9)-a\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =12-7a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, setting the expression for the determinant equal to $0$ and solving for $a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-7a & =0 \\\\ 7a & =12 \\\\ a & =\\frac{12}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299867 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93e896be-b4a5-5d09-9cd8-a51857e21d85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93e896be-b4a5-5d09-9cd8-a51857e21d85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298190} :answer-field/id #uuid "53818104-8243-535a-a22d-6b989af29871" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298921}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "970e3fe6-140a-52ff-acfd-c8d9428562eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-320642" :question/problem "Expressed in degrees, $arccos\\,(-\\frac{1}{2})=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n{0}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and second quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\frac{1}{2}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative.\n\n![](images/ca/ca39c4cde40b99fd40a11746a31398d82814268b2df706e3101ee88648c9e2d4.png)\n\nFrom the values in the second quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\ncos\\,({120}^{∘})=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\narccos\\,(-\\frac{1}{2})={120}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299868 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfbaef3d-a823-5681-9091-775966de482e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfbaef3d-a823-5681-9091-775966de482e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298398} :answer-field/id #uuid "30eab0f5-b9cb-5630-9096-823572a5a916" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "95fd8937-bffa-5ef8-bddc-0ced3581f01a" :question/math-academy-id "q-320646" :question/problem "Expressed in radians, $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and second quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative.\n\n![](images/a3/a33ea7c6d1aa7cd7e54376aefb2af4347da59a61ecaabdbf4964d41b9661870a.png)\n\nFrom the values in the second quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299870 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b78806e-edbf-59f5-af84-20651d054cdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b78806e-edbf-59f5-af84-20651d054cdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298087} :answer-field/id #uuid "7382eb32-6613-5392-aedb-7befad1e2c19" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a983de3-38d8-5868-9695-96338b07b5e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-320657" :question/problem "Expressed in radians, $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and second quadrants.\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299872 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27c84ae7-cdfc-5650-829a-b89d72d920a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27c84ae7-cdfc-5650-829a-b89d72d920a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297720} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cfa6ed2-8f02-579f-a47b-061fc18a9d7f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298956}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "82b0ad06-80da-5022-a24d-7d7a5827c2fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-320667" :question/problem "Expressed in degrees, $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the values in the first quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡({60}^{∘})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})={60}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299881 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e23098a-292c-5e41-933d-2c943df2b8a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e23098a-292c-5e41-933d-2c943df2b8a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298025} :answer-field/id #uuid "a788ab8f-102d-593c-bb34-5731d47144fe" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299203} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d61aa4c-2794-518e-91b2-d0c09236624e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d61aa4c-2794-518e-91b2-d0c09236624e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297678} :answer-field/id #uuid "df5f2fd0-1b42-593e-8d73-e13d7f5a09c6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "de2328b7-9e15-5845-9b2b-25744372387c" :question/math-academy-id "q-320696" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the values of $\\theta $ at which the curve $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi $ intersects the line $2\\sqrt{3}\\,y=x$ are\n\n$\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,\\theta =$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the coordinates of the points of intersection, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $\\theta .$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3}\\,y & =x \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal value of $\\theta $ is\n\n$$\n\\theta =arctan\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nThis value lies inside the domain $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ so our first solution is ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nTo get a second solution, we add $\\pi $ to ${\\theta }_{1}:$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, our solutions are $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299885 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fab3ac45-bda0-58d0-b71e-93fdba69064c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fab3ac45-bda0-58d0-b71e-93fdba69064c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298621} :answer-field/id #uuid "56fc5489-ab59-59c7-a9b2-acb1096f06ca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298930}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e965292-76e8-5eea-83c9-7985bc2f61f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-320721" :question/problem "Expressed in radians, $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $1$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where the tangent is positive.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=1.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is an angle in the first quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299886 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cb41a8b-b096-5c45-89f8-cc8b0aaf7f71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297612}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cb41a8b-b096-5c45-89f8-cc8b0aaf7f71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297612} :answer-field/id #uuid "84f7ed51-4214-5e71-b8c1-61a54c5fb13b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ef8bf712-474f-56cc-83a0-2804b7e92977" :question/math-academy-id "q-320730" :question/problem "Expressed in degrees, $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}<\\theta <{90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the fourth quadrant, where the tangent is negative.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions and the oddness of the tangent function, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-{30}^{∘})=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $-{30}^{∘}$ is an angle in the fourth quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})={-30}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299896 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60373466-004c-5543-b101-269d5fd3cb62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60373466-004c-5543-b101-269d5fd3cb62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297957} :answer-field/id #uuid "b032bbbd-b845-5fd2-8a10-89f8e2008935" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "01eea586-0fb0-58da-b8d1-c0f9849fa6e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-320789" :question/problem "Expressed in radians, $\\frac{1}{2}arctan\\,(\\sqrt{3})=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $\\sqrt{3}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where the tangent is positive.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions, we know that \n\n$$\ntan\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is an angle in the first quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\narctan\\,(\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}arctan\\,(\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299906 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2303e32-8b26-55ec-9d0f-18e1bc3899cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(3){log}_{3}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298462}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2303e32-8b26-55ec-9d0f-18e1bc3899cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(3){log}_{3}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298462} :answer-field/id #uuid "792dd6e3-1db8-586c-a5f7-0da2170dac92" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d825ca35-2305-5442-afb6-a99517062893" :question/math-academy-id "q-320814" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={3}^{x}{log}_{3}⁡(x),$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $+\\,\\frac{{3}^{x}}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={3}^{x}$ and $v(x)={log}_{3}⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & ={({3}^{x})}^{′}{log}_{3}⁡(x)+{3}^{x}{({log}_{3}⁡(x))}^{′} \\\\  & =({3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3))({log}_{3}⁡(x))+({3}^{x})(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}) \\\\  & ={3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3){log}_{3}⁡(x)+\\frac{{3}^{x}}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299907 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca46b1c5-ece7-5119-abe8-777dcc7ec3b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{4}^{x}}{x\\operatorname{ln}(4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca46b1c5-ece7-5119-abe8-777dcc7ec3b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{4}^{x}}{x\\operatorname{ln}(4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298433} :answer-field/id #uuid "0aa44b57-7202-5d5a-9c5f-cd88604e7d86" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6161762d-7ca8-5487-95de-c569a1e07882" :question/math-academy-id "q-320815" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={4}^{x}{log}_{4}⁡(x),$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)={4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4){log}_{4}⁡(x)\\,+$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={4}^{x}$ and $v(x)={log}_{4}⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & ={({4}^{x})}^{′}{log}_{4}⁡(x)+{4}^{x}{({log}_{4}⁡(x))}^{′} \\\\  & =({4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4))({log}_{4}⁡(x))+({4}^{x})(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}) \\\\  & ={4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4){log}_{4}⁡(x)+\\frac{{4}^{x}}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299908 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e34c232-975f-51cf-8ed5-ecf0f78eff8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{5}^{-x}\\operatorname{ln}(5){log}_{7}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297797} {:answer/id #uuid "8a591582-6e6f-4147-9d4d-ddb81a77153c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5^{-x}\\ln5\\left(\\log_7x\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186576151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e34c232-975f-51cf-8ed5-ecf0f78eff8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{5}^{-x}\\operatorname{ln}(5){log}_{7}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297797} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b389b0c-f96f-5746-b4d1-da5e3f5fb10c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "42b535b3-90b5-5bc5-bbb3-36d4082e369c" :question/math-academy-id "q-320816" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={5}^{-x}{log}_{7}⁡(x),$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $+\\,\\frac{{5}^{-x}}{xln⁡(7)}$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={5}^{-x}$ and $v(x)={log}_{7}⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & ={({5}^{-x})}^{′}{log}_{7}⁡(x)+{5}^{-x}{({log}_{7}⁡(x))}^{′} \\\\  & =(-{5}^{-x}ln⁡(5))({log}_{7}⁡(x))+({5}^{-x})(\\frac{1}{xln⁡(7)}) \\\\  & =-{5}^{-x}ln⁡(5){log}_{7}⁡(x)+\\frac{{5}^{-x}}{xln⁡(7)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299914 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20b7d4fd-93df-5629-a60e-eed072667330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-240" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20b7d4fd-93df-5629-a60e-eed072667330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-240" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297691} :answer-field/id #uuid "c699808b-fa6d-529a-91ab-1db1d7cc2d25" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299303}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d45e6ce8-b2ee-563e-a4c3-f801246a5e65" :question/math-academy-id "q-320888" :question/problem "Expressed in degrees, $4\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](images/ca/ca39c4cde40b99fd40a11746a31398d82814268b2df706e3101ee88648c9e2d4.png)\n\nFrom the values on the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡({300}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, ${300}^{∘}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with ${300}^{∘}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract ${360}^{∘}.$\n\n$$\n{300}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{60}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=-{60}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) & =4\\cdot (-{60}^{∘}) \\\\  & ={-240}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299916 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "451b6dd9-5462-5c40-81a0-8f4506076c62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "451b6dd9-5462-5c40-81a0-8f4506076c62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297836} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c8dc542-2f0e-5367-a897-b31c1cf81b76" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c57ca0c5-d54f-5168-9beb-5c05c79c12db" :question/math-academy-id "q-320893" :question/problem "Expressed in degrees, $4\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](images/ca/ca39c4cde40b99fd40a11746a31398d82814268b2df706e3101ee88648c9e2d4.png)\n\nFrom the values on the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, ${315}^{∘}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with ${315}^{∘}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract ${360}^{∘}.$\n\n$$\n{315}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) & =4\\cdot (-{45}^{∘}) \\\\  & ={-180}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299922 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "816fa6c2-f991-5039-a41c-4fa48bc8c757" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "816fa6c2-f991-5039-a41c-4fa48bc8c757" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298109} :answer-field/id #uuid "059e198d-f61d-5e53-b612-06a829c8b2fd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4923000b-5c5c-55b7-9b95-5a7082500328" :question/math-academy-id "q-320946" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n{{field-1}}$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299927 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1552cb6c-5622-5577-bd40-d5ad4231f48f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297640}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1552cb6c-5622-5577-bd40-d5ad4231f48f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297640} :answer-field/id #uuid "f50c87a8-d765-563d-bc7f-05ccab0605f9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7e55b33f-c2bb-5990-8ae0-14349507bd3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-321100" :question/problem "Expressed in radians, $arcsin\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](images/a3/a33ea7c6d1aa7cd7e54376aefb2af4347da59a61ecaabdbf4964d41b9661870a.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\nsin\\,(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n\\frac{7\\pi }{4}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\narcsin\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299928 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8503fdd5-b8df-5855-aa50-f032f6d9c3a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298127}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8503fdd5-b8df-5855-aa50-f032f6d9c3a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298127} :answer-field/id #uuid "97d7ea95-40e1-5a4a-9535-40af137627ae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299140}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d69d5404-b3a2-5817-ae30-04fc0f7ba2c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-321120" :question/problem "Expressed in radians, $9\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](images/a3/a33ea7c6d1aa7cd7e54376aefb2af4347da59a61ecaabdbf4964d41b9661870a.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n\\frac{5\\pi }{3}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\narcsin\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9arcsin\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) & =9(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =-3\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299929 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d45792f3-0a28-56c8-a67d-3d50eaf93eb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d45792f3-0a28-56c8-a67d-3d50eaf93eb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298464} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b761f73-9699-5009-890b-c604bb787aa6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298735} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fba2eb2c-542e-5741-9da4-b68fe7825059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(1+x)^{2}}{4{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fba2eb2c-542e-5741-9da4-b68fe7825059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(1+x)^{2}}{4{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298625} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb33573b-f770-5250-8223-d49dca5de7e1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce51648f-7c56-5fd1-a391-8983dd27aa88" :question/math-academy-id "q-321141" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2t+1},\\,\\,\\,y=(t+1)^{2},\\,t\\ne -\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x\\ne $\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2t+1}\\,⇒\\,2t+1=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{1}{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=\\frac{1}{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={(\\frac{1}{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}+1)}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2x}+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1+x}{2x})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1+x)^{2}}{4{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{(1+x)^{2}}{4{x}^{2}}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne -\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2t+1}$ we must have $x\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(1+x)^{2}}{4{x}^{2}},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299932 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67871740-2511-5309-983f-d94c00dcb871" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67871740-2511-5309-983f-d94c00dcb871" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297997} :answer-field/id #uuid "580383a8-d490-5b5e-8329-f3a941754ed8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298937}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2641ea1e-dafb-594a-8a7a-dc35ec436fc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-321269" :question/problem "Expressed in degrees, $arccos\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n{0}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and second quadrants.\n\nSince $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive.\n\n![](images/ca/ca39c4cde40b99fd40a11746a31398d82814268b2df706e3101ee88648c9e2d4.png)\n\nFrom the values in the first quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\narccos\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302042 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "859cacd7-5021-5714-8b7c-c7c18ad73a3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300632}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "859cacd7-5021-5714-8b7c-c7c18ad73a3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300632} :answer-field/id #uuid "774bc989-caf7-5a2a-bfab-9ca69ee4fb27" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301595} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5e99420-7491-5062-87bb-ee0a63b9ee4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5e99420-7491-5062-87bb-ee0a63b9ee4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300798} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ba3c015-b759-53fb-9917-fb16a142f241" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "309f8df2-ff1b-5330-aaa7-587c18b468d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-321306" :question/problem "Express $z=14(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}))$ in the form $z=x+y\\,\\text{i},$ where $x$ and $y$ are real numbers. Give the **exact** values of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-$\n{{field-2}}\n$i$" :question/worked-solution "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nFirst, we find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the values and expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =14(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})) \\\\  & =14(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-\\text{i}\\,\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{14\\sqrt{3}}{2}-\\text{i}\\,\\frac{14}{2} \\\\  & =7\\sqrt{3}-7\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302043 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "198804bf-af9a-5fc5-aa0a-80dfba4c5066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300073}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "198804bf-af9a-5fc5-aa0a-80dfba4c5066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300073} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5ddd749-cb3f-55d1-9232-9e4165ddb9b0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301821} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d05d2be4-10af-58b6-a6b7-5ce48f39fcb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d05d2be4-10af-58b6-a6b7-5ce48f39fcb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301001} :answer-field/id #uuid "d104c14d-f848-548d-9d29-6d5a082dd642" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "24514d85-9074-586d-bd86-5782009ad2cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-321307" :question/problem "Express $z=16(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}))$ in the form $z=x+y\\,\\text{i},$ where $x$ and $y$ are real numbers. Give the **exact** values of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-$\n{{field-2}}\n$i$" :question/worked-solution "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nFirst, we find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the values and expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =16(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})) \\\\  & =16(\\frac{1}{2}-\\text{i}\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{2}-\\text{i}\\,\\frac{16\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =8-8\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302044 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbdb1aa2-ee88-505b-b0f5-11a4884aebd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300902}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbdb1aa2-ee88-505b-b0f5-11a4884aebd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300902} :answer-field/id #uuid "50dddaf7-bda4-507c-a0ba-dc6c23800b73" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301492} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5026dc1f-44d6-553f-896e-a2ae4c316b78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5026dc1f-44d6-553f-896e-a2ae4c316b78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300381} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9354bb9-e5b5-562f-a405-f1896c8ec358" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cabafb25-d8ad-503c-847c-e869adfd1bcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-321308" :question/problem "Express $z=18(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}))$ in the form $z=x+y\\,\\text{i},$ where $x$ and $y$ are real numbers. Give the **exact** values of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+$\n{{field-2}}\n$i$" :question/worked-solution "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nFirst, we find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the values and expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =18(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})) \\\\  & =18(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\text{i}\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{18}{2}+\\text{i}\\,\\frac{18\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =-9+9\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302045 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "633c874c-66b0-5dc9-9deb-b2099d9d9e8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "633c874c-66b0-5dc9-9deb-b2099d9d9e8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300464} :answer-field/id #uuid "2eee240a-dc89-5352-95ca-e10ac4dfc669" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301373} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f94efa30-7642-5ade-9551-fe8db98ba682" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f94efa30-7642-5ade-9551-fe8db98ba682" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301193} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfc5f858-af5f-5935-a5b3-6e09633833ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301937}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d5d42d22-afab-5657-a00e-582139185cce" :question/math-academy-id "q-321309" :question/problem "Express $z=20(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}))$ in the form $z=x+y\\,\\text{i},$ where $x$ and $y$ are real numbers. Give the **exact** values of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+$\n{{field-2}}\n$i$" :question/worked-solution "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nFirst, we find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the values and expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =20(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =20(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\text{i}\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{20\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\text{i}\\,\\frac{20\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-10\\sqrt{2}+10\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302046 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9d96af1-063c-5a93-ae4c-fbf94d822feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300813}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9d96af1-063c-5a93-ae4c-fbf94d822feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300813} :answer-field/id #uuid "a258f07c-da2c-5ef5-a60c-3b82e992a21f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301748} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce45ba4b-7d8c-5fb0-bd44-a0bc831be2b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce45ba4b-7d8c-5fb0-bd44-a0bc831be2b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300990} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2251ba3-99cc-528d-a718-347e04a261c6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "38098932-4e7b-5400-bb4a-1a39003f645b" :question/math-academy-id "q-321310" :question/problem "Express $z=4\\sqrt{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}))$ in the form $z=x+y\\,\\text{i},$ where $x$ and $y$ are real numbers. Give the **exact** values of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+$\n{{field-2}}\n$i$" :question/worked-solution "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =4\\sqrt{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+\\text{i}\\,\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 3}{2}+\\text{i}\\,\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =6+2\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302047 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a04fd90-c804-5fd9-8fa1-659e2bb10ac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a04fd90-c804-5fd9-8fa1-659e2bb10ac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300077} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea9eff38-aeac-5cb1-9523-bc9bdbea8f7e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301971}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c913b645-6400-5a0c-a995-145873544dfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-321311" :question/problem "Express $z=7(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))$ in the form $z=x+y\\,\\text{i},$ where $x$ and $y$ are real numbers.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}\n$i$" :question/worked-solution "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =7(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =7(0+\\text{i}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =7\\cdot 0+7\\cdot \\text{i} \\\\  & =7\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302048 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c03cb41-8dae-5697-aca1-1503571603b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{12{x}^{2}}{25}+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c03cb41-8dae-5697-aca1-1503571603b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{12{x}^{2}}{25}+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299993} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc50beaf-4f1b-5232-ade9-c9d9974970f5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301930}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "746bed7b-e090-56a7-9ad1-4c0bfac39908" :question/math-academy-id "q-321314" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{5} \\\\ y=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the double-angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{5}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{6}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{6} & =2{(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}-1 \\\\ -\\frac{y}{6} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{25}-1 \\\\ y & =-6(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{25}-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{12{x}^{2}}{25}+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=-\\frac{12{x}^{2}}{25}+6.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302049 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16bbeffa-db1c-5b1d-ad63-3a7384e31729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300050}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16bbeffa-db1c-5b1d-ad63-3a7384e31729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300050} :answer-field/id #uuid "34ad1466-19e3-5aaa-b651-784e21d815fe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3eac432e-a9f4-5e40-ae91-1f28e72debdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-321318" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6} \\\\ y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the double-angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{5}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{5} & =2{(\\frac{x}{6})}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{5} & =2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36})-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{5} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{36}-1 \\\\ y & =5(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18}-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}-5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302054 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fdd1475-384a-5422-be05-881929c1958c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "330" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300276}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fdd1475-384a-5422-be05-881929c1958c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "330" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300276} :answer-field/id #uuid "0782e01c-2e6f-5a1c-9dad-dcf537586fc0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301245} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57198438-ae58-5ced-853e-28d175022bcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300416}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57198438-ae58-5ced-853e-28d175022bcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300416} :answer-field/id #uuid "aaa8678e-05da-542d-adae-2234c494f820" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2d054a95-51bc-5cb9-9dbe-815d03306268" :question/math-academy-id "q-321347" :question/problem "Find the solutions **in degrees** to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arctan\\,(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=-{30}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince $-{30}^{∘}$ is outside the given domain, it is not a solution.\n\nTo generate the solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={180}^{∘}+(-{30}^{∘})={150}^{∘}, \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}+(-{30}^{∘})={330}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=150\\,{\\,}^{∘}$ and $x=330\\,{\\,}^{∘}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302058 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ad07eac-4531-5e39-ba60-3955048b52f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ad07eac-4531-5e39-ba60-3955048b52f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300084} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ace053b-c257-5ef7-b1b8-ad2acacbefcf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301364} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2665b984-2caa-50b4-bc88-bddfbe523e2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2665b984-2caa-50b4-bc88-bddfbe523e2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300145} :answer-field/id #uuid "668ae0d5-ad90-55c4-b03b-4d6b7e3879d6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301549} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa75b670-46c2-5a24-9eea-0f9253228ccb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300816}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa75b670-46c2-5a24-9eea-0f9253228ccb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300816} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0aca5ef-02e5-540c-9440-d60fefe80029" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "091fc971-f53b-5795-a1ff-bf6281c0d800" :question/math-academy-id "q-321383" :question/problem "Express $z=\\frac{4}{1+\\text{i}\\sqrt{3}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give the **exact** values of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ in **radians** from $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(${{field-2}}$)+i\\operatorname{sin}⁡(${{field-3}}$))$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $1-\\text{i}\\sqrt{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{4}{1+\\text{i}\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1-\\text{i}\\sqrt{3})}{(1+\\text{i}\\sqrt{3})(1-\\text{i}\\sqrt{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-4\\text{i}\\sqrt{3}}{{1}^{2}-{(\\text{i}\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-4\\text{i}\\sqrt{3}}{1-(-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-4i\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\\\  & =1-\\sqrt{3}\\,i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=1-\\sqrt{3}\\,i,$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=1$ and $y=-\\sqrt{3},$ $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=2\\cdot \\,(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}))\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302059 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36dd9d9c-d1f0-518b-9b47-a6b3afa02f4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36dd9d9c-d1f0-518b-9b47-a6b3afa02f4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300229} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce2024b7-a71f-5b6f-ae78-cce7af1981ee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e1fe2936-ce2d-5b95-a874-57358178bd10" :question/math-academy-id "q-321384" :question/problem "![](images/af/afa5a80d73d9d8511cc35804277ad9dc3f1b62b2e1dbe3911d6ea9d665c3baa8.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(5,3),$ find the polar equation of the ray. Assume that the polar angle is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$\\theta =$ \n$\\operatorname{arctan}⁡($ {{field-1}} \n$).$" :question/worked-solution "The angle that the ray makes with the positive $x$-axis is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the ray lies in the first quadrant, the polar equation is  \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\,\\frac{3}{5}\\,).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302060 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e7c4336-a655-5ca4-b53b-787475eaf802" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e7c4336-a655-5ca4-b53b-787475eaf802" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300684} :answer-field/id #uuid "14edbe52-e8d2-57ab-a851-83206759e38a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301294} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2263a534-e5d1-58ac-89a8-1772bdf38fbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2263a534-e5d1-58ac-89a8-1772bdf38fbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300126} :answer-field/id #uuid "a382cf1f-148d-56f6-bdf0-d3aef72ce48d" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301754} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50d412ae-6892-55a2-8759-91c3ccf6ee84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\sqrt{29}}{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50d412ae-6892-55a2-8759-91c3ccf6ee84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\sqrt{29}}{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300383} :answer-field/id #uuid "eafb6899-5687-5f22-ba3b-6313936f42d8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a5dceea2-37ec-5d79-9349-768d5f27de4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-321385" :question/problem "Express $z=\\frac{9}{5+2\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give the **exact** value of $r$ and round $\\theta $ in **radians** from $[0,2\\pi )$ to **two decimal places**.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot (cos$\n$(${{field-2}}$)$\n$+\\,isin$\n$(${{field-3}}$))$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $5-2\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{9}{5+2\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9(5-2\\text{i})}{(5+2\\text{i})(5-2\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{45-18\\text{i}}{{5}^{2}-(2\\text{i})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{45-18\\text{i}}{{5}^{2}+(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{45-18\\text{i}}{29} \\\\  & =\\frac{45}{29}-\\frac{18}{29}\\,i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=\\frac{45}{29}-\\frac{18}{29}\\,i,$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{45}{29})}^{2}+{(\\frac{18}{29})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2025+324}{{29}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2349}}{29} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{29}}{29}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=\\frac{45}{29}$ and $y=-\\frac{18}{29},$ $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -arctan\\,(\\frac{|y|}{x}) \\\\  & =2\\pi -arctan\\,(\\frac{18}{45}) \\\\  & ≈5.90\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places.\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{9\\sqrt{29}}{29}\\cdot \\,(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5.90)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5.90)).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302062 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3625268-1369-5f41-a534-a0649eceb53b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3625268-1369-5f41-a534-a0649eceb53b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300856} :answer-field/id #uuid "20d409ef-248e-5eab-a01b-2a42296f4315" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301330} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2d9df89-b63c-55a8-bd14-62b985a73272" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2d9df89-b63c-55a8-bd14-62b985a73272" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301091} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4a0ae5c-3a22-5796-9f64-eda5a7edb186" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301951}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b94a700-bd85-58aa-bc0c-7585b2834748" :question/math-academy-id "q-321410" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=-3t+2,\\,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{t},\\,t\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x\\ne $\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=-3t+2\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{2-x}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=\\frac{2-x}{3}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2-x}{3})} \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{x-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{3}{x-2}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne 0$ and $x=-3t+2$ , we must have $x\\ne 2.$ \n\nTherefore, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{x-2},\\,x\\ne 2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302063 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08e64462-1873-527c-97d7-71858cd935a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08e64462-1873-527c-97d7-71858cd935a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299977} :answer-field/id #uuid "6023a8be-8b48-57b8-ac63-0a72f7c6b2ba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301533} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "053cf31d-bf3b-5e8e-b7f3-41eeceb5f463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299956}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "053cf31d-bf3b-5e8e-b7f3-41eeceb5f463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299956} :answer-field/id #uuid "905c2b65-d5e0-5d46-b370-c8ab90bbdc07" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301685} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df48145a-0c8a-5416-b553-50aafde6b411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301073}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df48145a-0c8a-5416-b553-50aafde6b411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301073} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2476875-c02f-5d4e-b65a-f0911730382a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301999}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "89a81dab-08e9-5bc4-b7bc-65bffdc14b31" :question/math-academy-id "q-321420" :question/problem "Express $z=\\frac{3}{1-\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give the **exact** values of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ in **radians** from $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(${{field-2}}$)+i\\operatorname{sin}⁡(${{field-3}}$))$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $1+\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{3}{1-\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1+\\text{i})}{(1-\\text{i})(1+\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1+\\text{i})}{{1}^{2}-{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1+\\text{i})}{1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1+\\text{i})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $y=\\frac{3}{2}$ are both positive, $z$ lies in the first quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\frac{3}{2}}{\\frac{3}{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302064 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf2b55df-9b82-5289-aa52-ca93401fe545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf2b55df-9b82-5289-aa52-ca93401fe545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300919} :answer-field/id #uuid "481cf864-60e6-5fbd-9440-3fbc24bf7b2c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301468} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e7afbbc-1ab4-58c8-ba60-118f316bd98f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e7afbbc-1ab4-58c8-ba60-118f316bd98f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300011} :answer-field/id #uuid "580f9bf7-ed2e-5130-a167-0c9266f3537d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301516} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8897463-bfba-5bb1-b48b-20964139c591" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8897463-bfba-5bb1-b48b-20964139c591" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301113} :answer-field/id #uuid "80cd1d89-8562-50db-a9b0-aa4e20b2509f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301633}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "384bec95-fb53-5352-981d-5e071dfd5a84" :question/math-academy-id "q-321424" :question/problem "Express $z=\\frac{6}{1+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give the **exact** values of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ in **radians** from $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(${{field-2}}$)+i\\operatorname{sin}⁡(${{field-3}}$))$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{6}{1+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(1-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})}{(1+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})(1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(1-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})}{{1}^{2}-(\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(1-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})}{1-(-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(1-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{27}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=\\frac{3}{2}>0$ and $y=-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}<0,$ $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{|y|}{x}) \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}}{\\frac{3}{2}}) \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}))\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302072 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53b2515b-c470-5df9-b5f0-72da0d8839dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25(y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300393}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53b2515b-c470-5df9-b5f0-72da0d8839dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25(y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300393} :answer-field/id #uuid "4339e742-90db-5fe5-8713-242c87bd162a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301454} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01f966e1-32ba-571c-8d2f-139d236a9efb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01f966e1-32ba-571c-8d2f-139d236a9efb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299939} :answer-field/id #uuid "d17ed819-e5df-5f2a-85be-61fbd0c56120" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df968d14-24c9-53a0-ac49-2683ed9a8cca" :question/math-academy-id "q-321592" :question/problem "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola \n\n$$\nx+2=5t,\\,y-2={t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{x+2}{5},\\,{t}^{2}=y-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{x+2}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=y-2$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{25} & =y-2 \\\\ {(x+2)}^{2} & =25(y-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302073 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be1a65c2-eca2-5b45-8663-ee7f50a9be04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be1a65c2-eca2-5b45-8663-ee7f50a9be04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300911} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bdfcd38-1e9d-57ca-9699-842624f624bf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301670} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92ccb25b-4703-517c-bdc3-21a06e7f11e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92ccb25b-4703-517c-bdc3-21a06e7f11e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300698} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dd53698-094d-5ef2-b9ca-9b0bcdb7a218" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "328224d2-452f-5f0c-ba2d-99488ec4490a" :question/math-academy-id "q-321613" :question/problem "Find the solutions **in radians** to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{3}{2}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ Round your answers to **one decimal place**.\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arctan\\,(\\frac{3}{2})=0.982\\,793…≈1.0\n$$\n\nSince $1.0$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=1.0.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\pi +0.982\\,793…≈4.1.\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are $x=1.0$ and $x=4.1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302074 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3305af8-bdd6-5e9a-896e-c45e93186eb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "189" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3305af8-bdd6-5e9a-896e-c45e93186eb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "189" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300786} :answer-field/id #uuid "28514f5b-8ffb-523d-b746-691f965f37a1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301354} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1acaff9-8720-5626-8966-d0cfd0f42b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301007}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1acaff9-8720-5626-8966-d0cfd0f42b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301007} :answer-field/id #uuid "b97bc96b-a6d2-5453-9c58-63570bef11c0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a823b8d4-ba47-5b33-ab00-0143319290ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-321622" :question/problem "Find the solutions **in degrees** to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{6}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ Round your answers to **the nearest integer**.\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arctan\\,(\\frac{1}{6})=9.4623{…}^{∘}≈{9}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${9}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={9}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={180}^{∘}+9.4623{…}^{∘}≈{189}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order and rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions are $x={9}^{∘}$ and $x={189}^{∘}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302078 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31115159-c0c6-5a84-9da5-7602b9a85e08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300200}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31115159-c0c6-5a84-9da5-7602b9a85e08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300200} :answer-field/id #uuid "60a46e3e-89e7-5209-a2d9-d26d0f0dd9af" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301535} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "873902a9-0243-56c6-bb8e-45da8ae5efc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "873902a9-0243-56c6-bb8e-45da8ae5efc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300643} :answer-field/id #uuid "b525c5f3-d9f1-5854-aa91-75a6954eff81" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8c3245e-cb7f-5699-9644-faf5634111e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-321725" :question/problem "Find the solutions **in radians** to the equation $4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-3=0$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ Round your answers to **one decimal place**.\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-3 & =0 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =3 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})=0.643\\,501…≈0.6\n$$\n\nSince $0.6$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=0.6.$\n\nTo generate solutions in the domain, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\pi +0.643\\,501…≈3.8.\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are $x=0.6$ and $x=3.8.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302085 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13fcb247-f440-55a2-9bf7-cc1d06887442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13fcb247-f440-55a2-9bf7-cc1d06887442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300039} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e77d3c5-f86d-5bff-8127-98400c1e9a4f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "521c20c6-8032-5c25-9cc6-c7967c4bc8d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-321877" :question/problem "![](images/36/369954f7abfabce27d112287ed232ce9a3a7e75ce993bba6300203777442f8fc.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(2,2),$ find the polar equation of the ray. Assume that the polar angle is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The angles that the ray makes with the positive $x$-axis is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\theta =arctan\\,(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =arctan\\,(\\frac{2}{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the ray lies in the first quadrant, its polar equation is\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302086 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db87a9e9-814b-5244-b7e1-633df8f1840c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db87a9e9-814b-5244-b7e1-633df8f1840c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301049} :answer-field/id #uuid "3416650a-306d-58b1-a5f0-d1d046fd2a8a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301391} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65cede9d-f74e-5efd-a821-e817e6ba88d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65cede9d-f74e-5efd-a821-e817e6ba88d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300474} :answer-field/id #uuid "382ee829-97fd-5ba7-958a-3e2945045d4f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e6f26b9c-d6be-5728-81f7-ec89b388ce38" :question/math-academy-id "q-321879" :question/problem "Find the **exact solutions in radians** to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}} and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arctan\\,(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\pi +{x}_{1}=\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302092 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "190c7ee4-ad66-516f-b4e0-1bb3a04e087e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300071}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "190c7ee4-ad66-516f-b4e0-1bb3a04e087e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300071} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d7550ba-a365-5860-9c81-1f23c87d9caa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301324}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0393dc3c-4161-5478-9a2f-cbfba3f335eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-321898" :question/problem "![](images/b0/b07f18c13e3bca94d2b55d61b4a63b2a294d0e47ed8a2853f5e10c5140047b27.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(-3\\sqrt{3},-3),$ find the polar equation of the ray. Assume that the polar angle is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the reference angle associated with the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-3}{-3\\sqrt{3}}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the ray lies in the third quadrant. So, we find the angle in the third quadrant that has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302093 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "897f3f0b-a588-5471-a52f-a424811c8e45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "897f3f0b-a588-5471-a52f-a424811c8e45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300657} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d9ede85-5773-5b6f-8fc6-d1d365cc2324" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301424} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2daf815d-258e-5d34-bd23-854fa3794503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2daf815d-258e-5d34-bd23-854fa3794503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300183} :answer-field/id #uuid "9415610a-525b-5d6f-8a5e-df2545146639" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301700}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9029a697-8d69-532b-b921-5f4702dab115" :question/math-academy-id "q-321918" :question/problem "![](images/9a/9af86b077257cd7a6052de0a0a89d720935eace0ac87dd0bf1afc581ce84c60d.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=5$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ intersect at the point $Q,$ as shown above. The Cartesian coordinates of $Q$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "The polar coordinates of $Q$ are $(r,\\theta )=(5,\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $Q$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2.5 \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $Q$ are $(\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302100 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b09d730-d6f8-5cf7-a8bf-f99e04015682" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300086}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b09d730-d6f8-5cf7-a8bf-f99e04015682" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300086} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dbb153e-6b4d-545b-afde-8ca7a7eb19fb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301266} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e6aa180-7dbf-516f-b362-4b746a2f18e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e6aa180-7dbf-516f-b362-4b746a2f18e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300266} :answer-field/id #uuid "73e86db1-e1dd-57f3-bff4-6c02780d1fdf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1036ad5f-680e-537b-8426-836a619b5091" :question/math-academy-id "q-321970" :question/problem "![](images/5e/5e29d99fc6ae3a848bfe59ab9e77595ab9ca2a605d0d03139e8599adf15e6fd9.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=9$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. The Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(9,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =9cos\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =9\\cdot -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =9sin\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302101 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "801e3126-6f97-55f1-ad47-e2afc002d4ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "801e3126-6f97-55f1-ad47-e2afc002d4ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300597} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1fbc73a-3d60-559a-9cc5-5a1225fc2fc9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301746} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23db9de0-9618-545f-95a4-aa33750b9019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23db9de0-9618-545f-95a4-aa33750b9019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300132} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9f0bfc6-692d-528d-8a4b-122802a5cc69" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "53021125-fcea-5da1-87cd-be2698ee5417" :question/math-academy-id "q-321972" :question/problem "![](images/6a/6aeb571c312e4dcc0f9ffc4fe2ad9b1a1db5cf02a2f757b66191e31337a8c498.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=6\\sqrt{2}$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. The Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(6\\sqrt{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\,cos\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-6 \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\,sin\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-6,6).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302107 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7259ca90-ac7f-5204-b76c-f880b6d47cb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300530}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7259ca90-ac7f-5204-b76c-f880b6d47cb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300530} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c2bdfc5-b175-505a-a33a-1b3069be5ed8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301479} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b904d72-58f1-5c9b-9e17-2549649c8ec0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b904d72-58f1-5c9b-9e17-2549649c8ec0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300352} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e3d1b64-5bee-525b-af14-53cf89662b98" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301570} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6609f30-0d51-5c8d-a91b-58292949a7b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300872}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6609f30-0d51-5c8d-a91b-58292949a7b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300872} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2f1245d-c523-5365-86f8-ce50d2dc2115" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "260fee5a-9ecb-5e89-bd59-2ddce1d08a7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-322283" :question/problem "Assuming that $n$ is an integer, the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nx={\\theta }_{1}+bn,\\,x={\\theta }_{2}+bn,\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2}.$ Find the values of ${\\theta }_{1}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}$ in $[0,2\\pi ),$ and the value of $b.$\n\n${\\theta }_{1}=${{field-1}}$,\\,{\\theta }_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,b=$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $4$th quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](images/5a/5af1febb2c8dbf1b19ee64addd8f8ccdd5a30f9330d36ab800475fce9f6740c2.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution for the given equation is \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nWriting this as $x=\\theta +2n\\pi ,$ where $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ we get\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2},$ we have ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4},$ ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{7\\pi }{4},$ and $b=2\\pi .$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302110 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48cb0e9c-99a8-5c76-94e7-1da9d41fc81e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48cb0e9c-99a8-5c76-94e7-1da9d41fc81e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300332} :answer-field/id #uuid "f657407c-1496-5c4b-848f-c06eeb41b835" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f1cf4030-03f9-5725-9abc-7ed81f76c1a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-322566" :question/problem "![](images/02/024a7e545d37295878d7f32efd393310dcf1c2c9f88f124ffc5fe46b05155e2c.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and the line $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.$\n\nThe **exact** value of the $x$-coordinate of the point $A$ is \n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The $y$-values of both functions are the same at the intersection point. Equating the two equations gives:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arctan\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nThis value lies outside $-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<x<-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ To get a value that's inside the required domain, we add $-\\pi $ to the principal value. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =x+(-\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302120 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2515e9b-efd8-503d-8c67-d0572c0252ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "315" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300852}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2515e9b-efd8-503d-8c67-d0572c0252ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "315" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300852} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bfc13a0-0d34-5095-8709-ea1bd2484b65" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301317} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2f95d6b-3698-5fe2-b0b9-215b4abffb13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "360" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300782}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2f95d6b-3698-5fe2-b0b9-215b4abffb13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "360" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300782} :answer-field/id #uuid "85e41128-8160-5ccb-8730-1ead867ee566" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301648} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba0d64bf-0aa2-5840-865c-48e0da074069" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300892}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba0d64bf-0aa2-5840-865c-48e0da074069" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300892} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c24499d-c462-5c5c-ac12-bcb1eff37d48" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301732}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1dc540e5-57ae-510b-9053-51f05e597d8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-322656" :question/problem "Assuming that $n$ is an integer, the general solution to the equation $3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-6=0$ is given by \n\n$$\nx={\\theta }_{1}+bn,\\,x={\\theta }_{2}+bn,\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2}.$ Find the values of ${\\theta }_{1}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}$ in $[0,{360}^{∘}),$ and the value of $b.$\n\n${\\theta }_{1}=${{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,{\\theta }_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘},\\,b=$\n{{field-3}}${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-6 & =0 \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =6 \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =\\frac{6}{3\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\text{arccos}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/cf/cf16066cb8b8368129be39a22f5c15727ac698f44d6f870511c2e07ec317d643.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx={45}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}n,\\,x={315}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2},$ we have ${\\theta }_{1}=45\\,{\\,}^{∘},$ ${\\theta }_{2}=315\\,{\\,}^{∘},$ and $b=360\\,{\\,}^{∘}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302121 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f9cc18f-21e8-50d6-8280-c21de7bc3150" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300024}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f9cc18f-21e8-50d6-8280-c21de7bc3150" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300024} :answer-field/id #uuid "6406b312-e5c8-5e85-89eb-c0e6bc42b89b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301546} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "381f0c99-1e40-5da7-9c4e-21f5ad6dd753" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "381f0c99-1e40-5da7-9c4e-21f5ad6dd753" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300233} :answer-field/id #uuid "a233f29f-b939-5649-84d3-849ec5c73dfa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301747} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9a47e3d-9197-505f-a7e6-6061378afb2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9a47e3d-9197-505f-a7e6-6061378afb2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301194} :answer-field/id #uuid "d88b2241-c8fc-57e0-b788-b7e7a905dddc" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2781cad1-5251-5d9e-95c8-df9b823a321e" :question/math-academy-id "q-322666" :question/problem "Assuming that $n$ is an integer, the general solution to the equation $3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-6=0$ is given by \n\n$$\nx={\\theta }_{1}+bn,\\,x={\\theta }_{2}+bn,\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2}.$ Find the values of ${\\theta }_{1}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}$ in $[0,2\\pi ),$ and the value of $b.$\n\n${\\theta }_{1}=${{field-1}}$,\\,{\\theta }_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,b=$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-6 & =0 \\\\ 3\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =6 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arcsin\\,(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the $2$nd quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](images/b0/b0738a8a505b89ff687cb4de350a00f2e10d6209fbeee7dcdfdf0ee359a9cc3c.png)\n\nSince the second solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2},$ we have ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\text{ }{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\text{ }\\text{and}\\text{ }b=2\\pi .$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302122 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4581840b-3d29-57b4-a333-d40d9add8ce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4581840b-3d29-57b4-a333-d40d9add8ce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300309} :answer-field/id #uuid "114978bb-e758-5eac-93fe-4c98a046b605" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301283} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25fe5dcd-4ace-531c-a89e-536e66053313" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{146}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300144}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25fe5dcd-4ace-531c-a89e-536e66053313" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{146}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300144} :answer-field/id #uuid "47f133b3-10c8-5782-81b0-f846d0f7d4f5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301464} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8563b667-c254-556e-810f-72d65eb0b597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300629}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8563b667-c254-556e-810f-72d65eb0b597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300629} :answer-field/id #uuid "9de03a0e-a212-5bfe-ad02-23b7393e3deb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ad8e92c-0ebd-5c39-8d19-d9dce92fcbc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-322670" :question/problem "Express $z=5-11\\text{i}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give the **exact** value of $r$ and round $\\theta $ in **radians** from $[0,2\\pi )$ to **two decimal places**.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(${{field-2}}$)+i\\operatorname{sin}⁡(${{field-3}}$))$" :question/worked-solution "In polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=5-11\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(-11)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+121} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{146}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=5$ is positive and $y=-11$ is negative, $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-11}{5}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -arctan\\,(\\frac{11}{5}) \\\\  & ≈5.14,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places. \n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z=5-11\\text{i}$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{146}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5.14)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5.14))\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302123 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e6ba4e7-ec53-5571-b404-d7ae8fed7aaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300370}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e6ba4e7-ec53-5571-b404-d7ae8fed7aaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300370} :answer-field/id #uuid "021e74f2-cbf1-5b98-b2ba-3fa1e24972e6" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301238} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "859e57c5-85b4-5628-998b-9a784a91ac0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "859e57c5-85b4-5628-998b-9a784a91ac0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300633} :answer-field/id #uuid "1daae901-9a53-5d34-b502-7c5af4fe8a96" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301326} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ab4ccfa-051a-593c-a805-70f1d216c94c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300739}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ab4ccfa-051a-593c-a805-70f1d216c94c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300739} :answer-field/id #uuid "50afb390-5bc8-5b51-9edb-ccec40f173bb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301490}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf8141f3-3d6b-54e7-81c4-c29188080b02" :question/math-academy-id "q-322671" :question/problem "Express $z=\\sqrt{3}-\\text{i}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give the **exact** values of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ in **radians** from $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(${{field-2}}$)+\\,i\\operatorname{sin}⁡(${{field-3}}$))$" :question/worked-solution "In polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=\\sqrt{3}-\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $ represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=\\sqrt{3}>0$ and $y=-1<0,$ $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{3}}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -arctan\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z=\\sqrt{3}-\\text{i}$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}))\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302124 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "634badc8-2901-5d3c-8ed8-499bf0a2f8ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300465}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "634badc8-2901-5d3c-8ed8-499bf0a2f8ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300465} :answer-field/id #uuid "a68d2433-e88a-574d-8b1e-5758f18355af" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1f9978e0-d89e-56b3-a4e1-ec22c1dfd203" :question/math-academy-id "q-322701" :question/problem "![](images/e0/e03611d72e1c88d1e49806ede117b6808186de27346e426104dca69391f29bbf.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and the line $y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.$\n\nThe **exact** value of the $x$-coordinate of the point $A$ is \n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The $y$-values of both functions are the same at the intersection point. Equating the two equations gives:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arctan\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nThis value lies outside $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<x<\\frac{5\\pi }{2}.$ To get a value that's inside the required domain, we add $2\\pi $ to the principal value. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =x+2\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302132 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe734d3c-a68d-5020-a74a-79586931c9a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe734d3c-a68d-5020-a74a-79586931c9a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301221} :answer-field/id #uuid "2800a387-3f3c-5f66-8bb9-39e8d1d777b9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301353} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2af3c58-66b9-5a17-8512-fd10e7c48d2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2af3c58-66b9-5a17-8512-fd10e7c48d2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300853} :answer-field/id #uuid "49da97c4-6c8a-5133-b502-b1d790c52374" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cfbb5ea9-8628-5c66-9846-4b5cf013f95b" :question/math-academy-id "q-323209" :question/problem "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola\n\n$$\nx+7=-3t,\\,y-2=-9{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-\\frac{x+7}{3},\\,{t}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{9}(y-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{x+7}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+7)^{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{9}(y-2)$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+7)^{2}}{9} & =-\\frac{1}{9}(y-2) \\\\ {(x+7)}^{2} & =-(y-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302135 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd8366d8-1cd5-5f5a-988b-73bbff24427a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd8366d8-1cd5-5f5a-988b-73bbff24427a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301059} :answer-field/id #uuid "31d42bab-c07f-535d-841d-dd805a4bb990" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301385} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8be3abb-83fc-5bd0-a9e4-fe4aa693e868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8be3abb-83fc-5bd0-a9e4-fe4aa693e868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301116} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd5a93b4-152c-51eb-82f5-a66d7df77bc3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302037}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "254255ae-484c-589a-98c3-50d15ffe156c" :question/math-academy-id "q-323216" :question/problem "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola \n\n$$\nx={t}^{2},\\,y-3=5t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(y\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =x,\\,t=\\frac{y-3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{y-3}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=x$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{25} & =x \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =25x \\\\ {(y-3)}^{2} & =25x\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302138 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0a01899-364f-52e9-a345-8cb89103fff1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0a01899-364f-52e9-a345-8cb89103fff1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301078} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d5acff5-e535-5da1-ad3d-fcaaaf58bde8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301618} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d51731e-ad34-544d-a576-9afee4e3b849" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300754}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d51731e-ad34-544d-a576-9afee4e3b849" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300754} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfc6abed-315f-5ed2-8ebb-06ce8c5e8eca" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "40102566-3303-5239-a703-c00789ea4ec7" :question/math-academy-id "q-323221" :question/problem "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola \n\n$$\nx+2=4{t}^{2},\\,y-1=8t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(y\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{x+2}{4},\\,t=\\frac{y-1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{y-1}{8})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{x+2}{4}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{64} & =\\frac{x+2}{4} \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =64\\cdot (\\frac{x+2}{4}) \\\\ {(y-1)}^{2} & =16(x+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302140 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c521b91-4100-5e1b-aea0-ec183854127a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c521b91-4100-5e1b-aea0-ec183854127a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300507} :answer-field/id #uuid "48c71f97-8924-5064-a3cd-c59a9ef3953a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301471} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa838241-400e-5a94-ace1-75fc7c441362" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301199}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa838241-400e-5a94-ace1-75fc7c441362" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301199} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fea64cd-4cab-5371-b006-1b70758df91c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0901ad83-94a2-5f71-b9b0-e0800a27b5d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-323229" :question/problem "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola \n\n$$\nx-3=-{t}^{2},\\,y+4=-2t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(y\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-(x-3),\\,t=-\\frac{y+4}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{y+4}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-(x-3)$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{4} & =-(x-3) \\\\ (y+4)^{2} & =-4(x-3) \\\\ {(y+4)}^{2} & =-4(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302151 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06b727ac-e6d0-5937-a2a9-900eb69c6a2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1/2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299965}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06b727ac-e6d0-5937-a2a9-900eb69c6a2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1/2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299965} :answer-field/id #uuid "170d62b2-6193-5523-be19-aead18548068" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301302} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c74673e-c2ab-5889-8e3e-bec5faa3947d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5/2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c74673e-c2ab-5889-8e3e-bec5faa3947d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5/2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300508} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dbaa290-9941-5934-8411-2ce0c546c610" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301425} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59ca4adc-f255-5b34-b3db-ea777ee49a2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3/8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59ca4adc-f255-5b34-b3db-ea777ee49a2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3/8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300429} :answer-field/id #uuid "8757540e-8f4f-5260-abd1-525b3d5be69f" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301653} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11135029-ca34-5b73-ac2c-a6935c3fa9d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13/2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11135029-ca34-5b73-ac2c-a6935c3fa9d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13/2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300029} :answer-field/id #uuid "c61a5806-8cd6-5114-a5b2-a3cf868806f1" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301871} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "984e3170-b647-5a8d-ac22-b92f42b05a13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300726}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "984e3170-b647-5a8d-ac22-b92f42b05a13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300726} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec91e54a-571e-5963-ba4c-d762c39f850f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a3d03c74-03cc-5bc6-84c9-29bb98c3c0ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-323879" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3x-4y,\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the modified Euler method. Complete the first row of the table below, given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe modified Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the initially predicted slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3x-4y \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |  |  |\n\nThe coordinates of the initially predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=2+(-\\frac{5}{2})=-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |\n\nSo, the predicted slope at this point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {x}_{\\text{new}}-4\\cdot {y}_{p} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}-4\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{13}{2}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-5+\\frac{13}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{13}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ |"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302152 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93011b54-b30d-5d46-8ff2-f6022adb9e60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93011b54-b30d-5d46-8ff2-f6022adb9e60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300700} :answer-field/id #uuid "683942f2-71ae-5f63-8332-5d60fac43fc5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301554} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "239eb865-fc3b-5cd7-9cf7-6f5da57603c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "239eb865-fc3b-5cd7-9cf7-6f5da57603c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300131} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a1324c5-558d-5f6e-beeb-c47d31fdf323" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301662} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "852b299f-33ea-5d64-9868-fea936c065df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "852b299f-33ea-5d64-9868-fea936c065df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300627} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4fdc424-7103-5e35-8737-019e0713b20b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "58a0a3a9-3419-5548-9c90-737710ae4aa7" :question/math-academy-id "q-324056" :question/problem "![](images/6f/6f3382db4b66df920c22c7050ea5e22c56892094c51d8889872b7bfcb541f684.png)\n\nThe graph shows a portion of the curve $y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and the line $y=-\\sqrt{2}.$ Find expressions of the form\n\n$$\nx={\\theta }_{1}+n\\cdot b,\\,x={\\theta }_{2}+n\\cdot b,\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2},$ ${\\theta }_{1},{\\theta }_{2}\\in [0,2\\pi ),$ $b$ is the period in radians, and $n$ is an integer, that generate the $x$-values of all of their points of intersection.\n\n${\\theta }_{1}=${{field-1}}$,\\,{\\theta }_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,b=$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arccos\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\pi -\\frac{3\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the $3$rd quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](images/ff/ff58f07acb93044b6bca56ca30fe5f3b8447603ca9cb6d120559d287c435b987.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solutions are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2},$ we have ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\text{ }{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\text{ }\\text{and}\\text{ }b=2\\pi .$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302153 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a74d48fa-fcb9-5f97-9dc4-abaa3ba03155" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300804}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a74d48fa-fcb9-5f97-9dc4-abaa3ba03155" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300804} :answer-field/id #uuid "2056d42e-c6d0-5386-8f5e-a91ca0f276a0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301327} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97da6e3f-e645-544c-9cfb-00fc7af9453e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97da6e3f-e645-544c-9cfb-00fc7af9453e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300725} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f3c4870-f369-5d3e-9075-fa1b8c7239d4" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301374} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93f3deb0-683d-5eba-acaa-7c512203acdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300706}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93f3deb0-683d-5eba-acaa-7c512203acdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300706} :answer-field/id #uuid "87e0189c-b478-5a47-a80a-20755c7fd69f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4ff70978-9a65-58ca-9d6e-6cfce9ac09ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-324057" :question/problem "![](images/43/435dd125850e3f97b5c3753a027f2db8718131ab499ef6711eaac637b1a022bd.png)\n\nThe graph shows a portion of the curve $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and the line $y=1.$ Find expressions of the form\n\n$$\nx={\\theta }_{1}+n\\cdot b,\\,x={\\theta }_{2}+n\\cdot b,\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2},$ ${\\theta }_{1},{\\theta }_{2}\\in [0,2\\pi ),$ $b$ is the period in radians, and $n$ is an integer, that generate the $x$-values of all of their points of intersection.\n\n${\\theta }_{1}=${{field-1}}$,\\,{\\theta }_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,b=$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arcsin\\,(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the $2$nd quadrant and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](images/b0/b0738a8a505b89ff687cb4de350a00f2e10d6209fbeee7dcdfdf0ee359a9cc3c.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solutions are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2},$ we have ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\text{ }{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\text{ }\\text{and}\\text{ }b=2\\pi .$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302155 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d32b7c1-2505-5658-a644-b28c0281b497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17/8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300099}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d32b7c1-2505-5658-a644-b28c0281b497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17/8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300099} :answer-field/id #uuid "7379b78a-11ad-5ae6-9c95-d6b1eee16ef3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "856142f4-de92-591a-b38f-6186187501e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-324128" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y({x}^{2}+1),\\,y(-1)=1\n$$\n\nUse the modified Euler method with one step to approximate $y(-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\n$y(-\\frac{1}{2})≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe modified Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the initially predicted slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=-\\frac{1}{2}-(-1)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =1((-1)^{2}+1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |  |  |  |\n\nThe coordinates of the initially predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=1+1=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nSo, the predicted slope at this point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={y}_{p}\\cdot (({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1) \\\\  & =2\\cdot ({(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2+\\frac{5}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{9}{8}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy=1+\\frac{9}{8}=\\frac{17}{8}.\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{9}{8}$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{17}{8}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(-\\frac{1}{2})≈\\frac{17}{8}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302157 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0db3f942-5a8e-5c2a-9795-e961f39580cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-9{t}^{2}}}\\,i-20{t}^{3}\\,j-\\frac{6}{{t}^{4}}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300006} {:answer/id #uuid "59f11bd6-b9db-576a-845f-5008a1c945f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{1-9{t}^{2}}}\\,i-20{t}^{3}\\,j-\\frac{6}{{t}^{4}}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300430} {:answer/id #uuid "5a1ab615-f3d9-5c6e-be11-88b45849bbe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{1-9{t}^{2}}}\\,i-5{t}^{3}\\,j-\\frac{6}{{t}^{4}}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300431} {:answer/id #uuid "8710fa72-9009-525d-ae96-e335042f6b29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}\\,i-20{t}^{3}\\,j+\\frac{6}{{t}^{4}}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300641} {:answer/id #uuid "b90224c9-aa56-5a7b-91b0-1356c9aead70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{1-{t}^{2}}\\,i-20{t}^{3}\\,j-\\frac{6}{{t}^{4}}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59f11bd6-b9db-576a-845f-5008a1c945f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{1-9{t}^{2}}}\\,i-20{t}^{3}\\,j-\\frac{6}{{t}^{4}}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300430} :answer-field/id #uuid "0eb0988d-ace0-5f09-b160-12ad3b3114c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d5868b7-9316-570b-b593-afbcd34e82e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-32417" :question/problem "Find the derivative of  $f(t)=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡3t\\,i-5{t}^{4}\\,j+\\frac{2}{{t}^{3}}\\,k$ with respect to $t.$" :question/worked-solution "We find the derivative of $f(t)$  by differentiating each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arccos}⁡3t)\\,i-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5{t}^{4})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{{t}^{3}})\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{1-9{t}^{2}}}\\,i-20{t}^{3}\\,j-\\frac{6}{{t}^{4}}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302158 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0473e6f1-e16b-5684-a2ca-a6b48b59dadd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{170}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299952}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0473e6f1-e16b-5684-a2ca-a6b48b59dadd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{170}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299952} :answer-field/id #uuid "784ea1ed-8855-5f4f-96f4-e2a98eca853f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301599} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "761fe7b4-a429-59a2-b604-8e121cbc7b27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.79" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "761fe7b4-a429-59a2-b604-8e121cbc7b27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.79" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300545} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ff00fd4-5fb2-5eff-a026-671c4a825372" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301629} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84f897a5-3c96-5387-8c43-644629270364" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.79" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84f897a5-3c96-5387-8c43-644629270364" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.79" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300626} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac995886-372e-55e8-9cc3-393ba6e69182" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "84132fd7-313c-5312-aa24-f8eea348cc26" :question/math-academy-id "q-324223" :question/problem "Express $z=\\frac{3-5\\text{i}}{4+2\\,\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give the **exact** value of $r$ and round $\\theta $ in **radians** from $[0,2\\pi )$ to **two decimal places**.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot (cos$ \n$(${{field-2}}$)$\n$+\\,isin$\n$(${{field-3}}$))$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $4-2\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{3-5\\text{i}}{4+2\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3-5\\text{i})(4-2\\text{i})}{(4+2\\text{i})(4-2\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{12-6\\text{i}-20\\text{i}+10{\\text{i}}^{2}}{{4}^{2}-(2\\text{i})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12-26\\text{i}-10}{16-(-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-26\\text{i}}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}-\\frac{13}{10}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=\\frac{1}{10}-\\frac{13}{10}\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{13}{10})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1+169}{100}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{170}{100}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{170}}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{170}}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=\\frac{1}{10}$ and $y=-\\frac{13}{10},$ $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{|y|}{x}) \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\frac{13}{10}}{\\frac{1}{10}}) \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(13) \\\\  & ≈2\\pi -1.49 \\\\  & ≈4.79,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places. \n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{\\sqrt{170}}{10}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4.79)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4.79))\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302163 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44a6b172-511b-59d5-a4be-e3d793af9fea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44a6b172-511b-59d5-a4be-e3d793af9fea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300302} :answer-field/id #uuid "510fc5f1-e97e-592f-b0a4-6e434ea14445" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301494} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61f632a9-2c61-532d-871f-cfc71a60aead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61f632a9-2c61-532d-871f-cfc71a60aead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300461} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcca370c-170d-50be-9a17-36e8d982d9c5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302034}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "215c16c3-5948-5417-9503-ddb002423a5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-324344" :question/problem "Express $z=8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}))$ in the form $z=x+y\\,\\text{i},$ where $x$ and $y$ are real numbers. Give the **exact** values of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-$\n{{field-2}}\n$i$" :question/worked-solution "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nFirst, we find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the values and expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =8(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\text{i}\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\text{i}\\,\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{2}-4\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302165 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c78ff14-c03c-56f1-be7f-58608c94e650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c78ff14-c03c-56f1-be7f-58608c94e650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300361} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a229b7b-9184-53e5-8fa6-8630a0038588" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301359} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "065a303f-5e11-53fc-a2f3-3bad07ac7eeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "065a303f-5e11-53fc-a2f3-3bad07ac7eeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299963} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a18d5f4-2049-5a72-9123-2c9febfd8716" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9ac6cfc5-8412-5ead-ae4f-e70cb354576c" :question/math-academy-id "q-324392" :question/problem "Find, **in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place**, the two solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{4}{3}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value of $x,$ rounded to three decimal places:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arccos\\,(\\frac{3}{4})≈0.723\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}≈0.723.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the $4$th quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}≈0.723.$\n\n![](images/3b/3b95f4a3d92d2214472f8c72f9322f6d1aa89a6e2c4f8cc2ae9f13015217a84d.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ≈2\\pi -0.723 \\\\  & ≈5.560\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are $x=0.7$ and $x=5.6.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302166 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "731758a4-7b51-54b2-9208-ce4122e6b908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "78.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300534}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "731758a4-7b51-54b2-9208-ce4122e6b908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "78.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300534} :answer-field/id #uuid "2349bb5b-a4de-53b5-8227-9a084342fdd7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301337} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e2de191-e974-5606-80cf-1ec2b7b1ba89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "281.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e2de191-e974-5606-80cf-1ec2b7b1ba89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "281.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300759} :answer-field/id #uuid "391f6007-94a5-5a49-b561-e1a56585ccc9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5c36479a-53a2-504d-8cbf-7d29626fa8b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-324396" :question/problem "Find, **in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place**, the two solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=5$ for $0\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value of $x,$ rounded to three decimal places:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arccos\\,(\\frac{1}{5})≈{78.463}^{∘}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}≈{78.463}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the $4$th quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}≈{78.463}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/e0/e05c5b50dc8308d278cb6dac3408349da1865d37601003e0ee3025c937404239.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ≈{360}^{∘}-{78.463}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈{281.537}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are $x=78.5\\,{\\,}^{∘}$\nand $x=281.5\\,{\\,}^{∘}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302167 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "667f8593-c404-5891-ad6f-768da4a13a28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "115.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300479}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "667f8593-c404-5891-ad6f-768da4a13a28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "115.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300479} :answer-field/id #uuid "4418f07e-f4a6-5926-a0bd-b1fedf38313c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301455} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbf3194f-f80f-544e-bbb7-87d30eddad8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "244.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301051}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbf3194f-f80f-544e-bbb7-87d30eddad8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "244.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301051} :answer-field/id #uuid "e63dcfb3-c5b6-5d40-8637-84587fd9e56a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301957}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "442bcaa4-6366-559d-86b7-c0022ac3924b" :question/math-academy-id "q-324397" :question/problem "Find, **in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place**, the two solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-2.3$ for $0\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2.3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value of $x,$ rounded to three decimal places:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arccos\\,(-\\frac{1}{2.3})≈{115.771}^{∘}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}≈{180}^{∘}-{115.771}^{∘}≈{64.229}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the $3$rd quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}≈{64.229}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/97/974fad6534267df035b759df9064ed7dfaff80cb299c41744e8888f248ed1eee.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ≈{180}^{∘}+{64.229}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈{244.229}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are $x=115.8\\,{\\,}^{∘}$ and $x=244.2\\,{\\,}^{∘}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302168 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "493dc1d7-927f-58a0-b73a-a76e636a0f2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "493dc1d7-927f-58a0-b73a-a76e636a0f2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300336} :answer-field/id #uuid "311c1232-bea5-56ca-8573-78b8c656d44e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301383} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "079a6ad9-2432-5c6c-b93f-214d73ad8ee6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299968}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "079a6ad9-2432-5c6c-b93f-214d73ad8ee6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299968} :answer-field/id #uuid "94fd2765-f411-5827-8751-81c105ddfe0f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ac93d942-8a4e-5c8d-b866-cfb74d41e336" :question/math-academy-id "q-324401" :question/problem "Find, **in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place**, the two solutions to the equation $5\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2\\sqrt{2}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\sqrt{2} & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-2\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{2}}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nThen, we find the principal value of $x,$ rounded to three decimal places:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arccos\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})≈2.062\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}≈\\pi -2.062≈1.080.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the $3$rd quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}≈1.080.$\n\n![](images/c8/c8020c19d17f3e79b825cb4e0cad4471e68ec0e2618a12578115a0fc8c014599.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ≈\\pi +1.080 \\\\  & ≈4.222\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are $x=2.1$ and $x=4.2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302169 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0a05661-7722-5963-b806-be7baa982a14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0a05661-7722-5963-b806-be7baa982a14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301079} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fef1f38-f1c6-5dcb-ab28-87a9ef1d2f28" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301488}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c76de2b6-dcfd-5626-8f87-83f3745840b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-324458" :question/problem "In electronics manufacturing, the average resistance of a certain type of resistor is $100\\,Ω.$ However, $5%$ of resistors have resistance no more than $96.5\\,Ω.$ Assuming that resistor resistances are normally distributed, find the standard deviation, $\\sigma ,$ of the resistance, **rounded to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $\\,p\\,$ | $0.25$ | $0.20$ | $0.15$ | $0.10$ | $0.05$ | $0.01$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,{Φ}^{-1}(p)\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$\\sigma ≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$Ω$" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the resistance (in ohms). Then we have $X∼N(100,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X\\le 96.5)=0.05.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 96.5) & =0.05 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{96.5-100}{\\sigma }) & =0.05 \\\\ Φ(-\\frac{3.5}{\\sigma }) & =0.05 \\\\ -\\frac{3.5}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.05).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.05)=-1.6449.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3.5}{\\sigma } & =-1.6449 \\\\ \\frac{3.5}{\\sigma } & =1.6449 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{3.5}{1.6449} \\\\  & ≈2.13\\,Ω.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302175 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b08128b-1f0b-5a20-a676-5e2f6c57e710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300248}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b08128b-1f0b-5a20-a676-5e2f6c57e710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300248} :answer-field/id #uuid "257f288c-307b-5385-a7d9-8419607f71c2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301343} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed5f4c45-3631-5885-bfe2-a19dc9739185" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301134}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed5f4c45-3631-5885-bfe2-a19dc9739185" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301134} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b8ccddc-3790-52b9-bbb6-e4975313ec73" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301728}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a7e25bbd-62e2-55a9-9433-daf79eb00d75" :question/math-academy-id "q-325107" :question/problem "Find the **exact** solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ for $-2\\pi <x<0.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arccos\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is not in the domain $-2\\pi <x<0.$ To get a solution within the required domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}-2\\pi =-\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](images/17/17803dc8d1d3ca2e31586969797b401d5d0a2d958bd6628387b512974076df7a.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the fourth quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $X=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ is not in the domain $-2\\pi <x<0.$ To get a solution within the required domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ and $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302179 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99ce7345-0881-54d7-963b-790e4b1b8812" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99ce7345-0881-54d7-963b-790e4b1b8812" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300735} :answer-field/id #uuid "395ae32e-ffd1-57d7-a4ad-7c42940cf5a7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301413} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88e8d3f5-6a59-55cd-a429-968af392b6a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88e8d3f5-6a59-55cd-a429-968af392b6a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300651} :answer-field/id #uuid "944f0584-ebf6-5588-9bbd-e29d582d93f1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9d3bce2a-acb5-5009-b608-6cf97a06fc87" :question/math-academy-id "q-325113" :question/problem "Find the two solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-2$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x<{180}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arccos\\,(-\\frac{1}{2})={120}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince $x={120}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={120}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={180}^{∘}-{120}^{∘}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/29/29560b074afbb7d4e0cde3c1964e14696ef9879618e42c0b0c41f478e7ffcb72.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the third quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={240}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution ${x}_{2}$ that's within the given domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={240}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{120}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-120\\,{\\,}^{∘}$ and $x=120\\,{\\,}^{∘}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302180 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4aaa2f26-f6e2-5045-99b6-6e56727e402e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44.83" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4aaa2f26-f6e2-5045-99b6-6e56727e402e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44.83" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300344} :answer-field/id #uuid "57d68a22-b87f-57cb-891e-6da9bbc1952a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301513}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d53565bf-d2c4-5e5a-8659-e7dab8f5253a" :question/math-academy-id "q-325116" :question/problem "The manufacturing times of bicycle frames are normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ minutes and a standard deviation of $5$ minutes. Additionally, $25%$ of frames are completed in under $48$ minutes. Find the percentage of frames that take longer than $52$ minutes. **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5517\\,$ | $\\,0.5557\\,$ | $\\,0.5596\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5910\\,$ | $\\,0.5948\\,$ | $\\,0.5987\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6293\\,$ | $\\,0.6331\\,$ | $\\,0.6368\\,$ |\n\nApproximately\n{{field-1}}$%$ of the frames take longer than $52$ minutes." :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the time required to manufacture a bicycle frame. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{5}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X>52).$ \n\nWe know that $P(X<48)=0.25.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<48) & =0.25 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{48-\\mu }{5}) & =0.25 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{48-\\mu }{5}) & =0.25 \\\\ \\frac{48-\\mu }{5} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.25)=-0.6745.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{48-\\mu }{5} & =-0.6745 \\\\ 48-\\mu  & =-3.3725 \\\\ \\mu  & =51.3725.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(52<X)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>52) & =P(Z>\\frac{52-51.3725}{5}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.13) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.13) \\\\  & =1-0.5517 \\\\  & ≈44.83%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $44.83%$ of the frames take longer than $52$ minutes."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302181 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2ad50e2-704b-5f15-8f59-8903e66cedf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2ad50e2-704b-5f15-8f59-8903e66cedf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300780} :answer-field/id #uuid "0595c7f4-2b1b-55b0-8eaf-405f478bd32c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301240} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c11d39fd-7d64-5d17-8d0b-23bc18cb4c95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300931}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c11d39fd-7d64-5d17-8d0b-23bc18cb4c95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300931} :answer-field/id #uuid "5243862f-5764-5836-b392-6aee0b0a590e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301498}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "eab8e489-594d-52e1-8e07-a54c47bfe885" :question/math-academy-id "q-325125" :question/problem "Find the two solutions to the equation $(1+\\sqrt{2})\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\sqrt{2}\\,(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1)$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x<{180}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1+\\sqrt{2})\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =\\sqrt{2}\\,(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1) \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arccos\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${45}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={45}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/cf/cf16066cb8b8368129be39a22f5c15727ac698f44d6f870511c2e07ec317d643.png)\n\nAnother solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution ${x}_{2}$ that's within the given domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={315}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{45}^{∘}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-45\\,{\\,}^{∘}$ and $x=45\\,{\\,}^{∘}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302182 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66f3bd62-ddb7-5adc-ba61-df9d1b1cefd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66f3bd62-ddb7-5adc-ba61-df9d1b1cefd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300481} :answer-field/id #uuid "e77eacb4-8703-5ba3-a8de-2e5f69a5491f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d7053778-cd34-5d92-bb60-d0cacb0e2655" :question/math-academy-id "q-325129" :question/problem "The resting heart rate of a group of adults is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu \\,\\text{bpm}$ and a standard deviation of $8\\,\\text{bpm}.$ Additionally, $70%$ of the adults have a resting heart rate between $0.9\\mu \\,\\text{bpm}$ and $1.1\\mu \\,\\text{bpm}.$ Find the percentage of adults with a resting heart rate lower than $70\\,\\text{bpm}.$ **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.0359\\,$ | $\\,0.0351\\,$ | $\\,0.0344\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.0446\\,$ | $\\,0.0436\\,$ | $\\,0.0427\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.0548\\,$ | $\\,0.0537\\,$ | $\\,0.0526\\,$ |\n\nApproximately\n{{field-1}}$%$ of the adults have a resting heart rate lower than $70\\,\\text{bpm}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the resting heart rate of a randomly selected adult. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{8}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<70).$ But first, we need to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.9\\mu <X<1.1\\mu )=0.70.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.9\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.70 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.70 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.35 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.5+0.35 \\\\ P(X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.85\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.85 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.1\\mu }{8}) & =0.85 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.1\\mu }{8}) & =0.85 \\\\ \\frac{0.1\\mu }{8} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.85).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.85)=1.0364.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.1\\mu }{8} & =1.0364 \\\\ 0.1\\mu  & =8.2912 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{8.2912}{0.1} \\\\  & ≈82.912.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<70)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<70) & =P(Z<\\frac{70-82.912}{8}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-1.61) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.61) \\\\  & =0.0537 \\\\  & ≈5.37%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $5.37%$ of the adults have a resting heart rate lower than $70\\,\\text{bpm}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302208 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95029773-683b-5385-a7da-6a99ac24d589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(x-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300709}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95029773-683b-5385-a7da-6a99ac24d589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(x-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300709} :answer-field/id #uuid "af2f2e35-302f-5905-8795-f59862d3e0c6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301799}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63cf2de0-063c-5351-b2a2-41102366bf48" :question/math-academy-id "q-325348" :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $(x-7)^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1.$\n\n$y=\\pm $\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at ((h,k)) is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a horizontal hyperbola, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k)=(7,0),\\,a=\\sqrt{1}=1,\\,b=\\sqrt{9}=3.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the asymptotes are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =\\pm (\\frac{3}{1})(x-7) \\\\ y & =\\pm \\,3(x-7).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302209 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5629aab9-927d-5e32-8a34-a668b8454955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}(x-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5629aab9-927d-5e32-8a34-a668b8454955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}(x-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300411} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4969692-dd44-52b9-9330-6fdce76d76e3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98dd5660-05fa-59d2-b033-e4a0cbe5c62f" :question/math-academy-id "q-325351" :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1.$\n\n$y=\\pm $\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a horizontal hyperbola, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k)=(5,0),\\,a=\\sqrt{9}=3,\\,b=\\sqrt{16}=4.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the asymptotes are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =\\pm (\\frac{4}{3})(x-5) \\\\ y & =\\pm \\,\\frac{4}{3}(x-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302210 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b8b4dc6-2350-5fc7-948a-7b159895567f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b8b4dc6-2350-5fc7-948a-7b159895567f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300669} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d7793d9-94dc-5c87-bf6e-61329702df92" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301529}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef2fa925-03fc-57fe-ab11-3002168fda88" :question/math-academy-id "q-325365" :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{12}-\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{27}=1.$\n\n$y=\\pm $\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a vertical hyperbola, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k)=(-3,0),\\,a=\\sqrt{12}=2\\sqrt{3},\\,b=\\sqrt{27}=3\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the asymptotes are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =\\pm (\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3\\sqrt{3}})(x-(-3)) \\\\ y & =\\pm \\,\\frac{2}{3}(x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302211 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99907e0d-b3e2-5c4a-bc30-f8ba0eb6c396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300730}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99907e0d-b3e2-5c4a-bc30-f8ba0eb6c396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300730} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f8dd51b-01fe-55c7-bb71-da3c7f07b300" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301625}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b48adc4e-2842-554c-ab62-d5dfcbbb84fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-325367" :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{27}-\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{12}=1.$\n\n$y=\\pm $\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a vertical hyperbola, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k)=(4,0),\\,a=\\sqrt{27}=3\\sqrt{3},\\,b=\\sqrt{12}=2\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the asymptotes are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =\\pm (\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{3}})(x-4) \\\\ y & =\\pm \\,\\frac{3}{2}(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302212 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da362cc3-0bd6-5f81-bc2e-8ed5929a0708" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da362cc3-0bd6-5f81-bc2e-8ed5929a0708" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301043} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f223efa-1358-5c45-a3bd-eac47544ea71" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301575} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b71c3e71-9fc7-512d-8462-2105c6deb7f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300876}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b71c3e71-9fc7-512d-8462-2105c6deb7f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300876} :answer-field/id #uuid "79e38438-b362-5d27-a9be-8fd124d0b2c4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13971822-6d4b-5c82-bab8-f512e49462e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-325369" :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18}=1.$\n\n$y\\,\\,+$\n{{field-1}}\n$=\\pm $\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a vertical hyperbola, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k)=(0,-4),\\,a=\\sqrt{8}=2\\sqrt{2},\\,b=\\sqrt{18}=3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the asymptotes are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-4) & =\\pm (\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{2}})(x-0) \\\\ y+4 & =\\pm \\,\\frac{2}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302213 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "177d7083-db2b-5816-9859-6a03a9cb5923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300057}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "177d7083-db2b-5816-9859-6a03a9cb5923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300057} :answer-field/id #uuid "b341bd78-8ac1-5b11-85f0-45cddef92012" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "390c13e9-a9fe-509a-bdd2-9adc4787d2ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-325375" :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $4{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}-24x=0.$\n\n$y=\\pm $\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Group the $x$ terms and complete the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}-24x & =0 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-6x)-9{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 4\\,[(x-3)^{2}-9]-9{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 4(x-3)^{2}-36-9{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 4(x-3)^{2}-9{y}^{2} & =36 \\\\ \\frac{4(x-3)^{2}}{36}-\\frac{9{y}^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus it is a horizontal hyperbola\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1,\n$$\n\nso the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}(x-h).\n$$\n\nHere,\n\n$$\n(h,k)=(3,0),\\,a=\\sqrt{9}=3,\\,b=\\sqrt{4}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =\\pm (\\frac{2}{3})(x-3) \\\\ y & =\\pm \\,\\frac{2}{3}(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302214 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a8cbc9a-fe82-5f16-9477-0ead4cbd6151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a8cbc9a-fe82-5f16-9477-0ead4cbd6151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300567} :answer-field/id #uuid "09b0fece-9027-5e28-84f8-a357385af28a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301254} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a309997-af4b-5ec5-b86d-ca37bad43fc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300163}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a309997-af4b-5ec5-b86d-ca37bad43fc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300163} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd52fc1a-ec64-5a6c-b228-f8368c77d7ca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301883}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aae5ce2a-45a6-528f-aca1-899841eaa337" :question/math-academy-id "q-325434" :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $25{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}-150x-8y+121=0.$\n\n$y\\,\\,+$\n{{field-1}}\n$=\\pm $\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Group the $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}-150x-8y+121 & =0 \\\\ 25({x}^{2}-6x)-4({y}^{2}+2y)+121 & =0 \\\\ 25\\,[(x-3)^{2}-9]-4\\,[(y+1)^{2}-1]+121 & =0 \\\\ 25(x-3)^{2}-225-4(y+1)^{2}+4+121 & =0 \\\\ 25(x-3)^{2}-4(y+1)^{2} & =100 \\\\ \\frac{25(x-3)^{2}}{100}-\\frac{4(y+1)^{2}}{100} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{25} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus it is a horizontal hyperbola\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1,\n$$\n\nso the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}(x-h).\n$$\n\nHere,\n\n$$\n(h,k)=(3,-1),\\,a=\\sqrt{4}=2,\\,b=\\sqrt{25}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-1) & =\\pm (\\frac{5}{2})(x-3) \\\\ y+1 & =\\pm \\,\\frac{5}{2}(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302215 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66e8137b-d51e-596e-be07-561b56b866c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66e8137b-d51e-596e-be07-561b56b866c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300480} :answer-field/id #uuid "5af9ecd4-abb7-5235-8c01-1d65156c6708" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301525} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "661af4c5-f722-5b50-b6e6-f2f5e9db13e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "661af4c5-f722-5b50-b6e6-f2f5e9db13e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300478} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2047f6c-42bd-50a1-b6fe-d1f66fafaaa6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ad30d958-fdac-5134-b95b-b0c6f52615f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-325436" :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $9{y}^{2}-4{x}^{2}+54y-27=0.$\n\n$y\\,\\,+$\n{{field-1}}\n$=\\pm $\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group the $x$ terms and complete the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{y}^{2}-4{x}^{2}+54y-27 & =0 \\\\ 9({y}^{2}+6y)-4{x}^{2}-27 & =0 \\\\ 9\\,[(y+3)^{2}-9]-4{x}^{2}-27 & =0 \\\\ 9(y+3)^{2}-81-4{x}^{2}-27 & =0 \\\\ 9(y+3)^{2}-4{x}^{2} & =108 \\\\ \\frac{9(y+3)^{2}}{108}-\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{108} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{12}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{27} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe asymptotes of a vertical hyperbola\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k)=(0,-3),\\,a=\\sqrt{12}=2\\sqrt{3},\\,b=\\sqrt{27}=3\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-3) & =\\pm (\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3\\sqrt{3}})(x-0) \\\\ y+3 & =\\pm \\,\\frac{2}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302216 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60ca79fe-b2fd-56f1-9067-ca26b85f3790" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60ca79fe-b2fd-56f1-9067-ca26b85f3790" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300459} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b528f26-d397-52fb-b3ae-1c4c89b276a2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301610} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9360f383-37b3-5f88-9f17-21acb8605c22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9360f383-37b3-5f88-9f17-21acb8605c22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300702} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3f95b7f-6363-57fe-86a4-b0f4ef3a5f01" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "586f88cb-07e5-56af-b3a5-889f8ba2856f" :question/math-academy-id "q-325439" :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $9{y}^{2}+36y-16{x}^{2}+32x-124=0.$\n\n$y\\,\\,+$\n{{field-1}}\n$=\\pm $\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group the $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{y}^{2}+36y-16{x}^{2}+32x-124 & =0 \\\\ 9({y}^{2}+4y)-16({x}^{2}-2x)-124 & =0 \\\\ 9\\,[(y+2)^{2}-4]-16\\,[(x-1)^{2}-1]-124 & =0 \\\\ 9(y+2)^{2}-36-16(x-1)^{2}+16-124 & =0 \\\\ 9(y+2)^{2}-16(x-1)^{2} & =144 \\\\ \\frac{9(y+2)^{2}}{144}-\\frac{16(x-1)^{2}}{144} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{9} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe asymptotes of a vertical hyperbola\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k)=(1,-2),\\,a=\\sqrt{16}=4,\\,b=\\sqrt{9}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-2) & =\\pm (\\frac{4}{3})(x-1) \\\\ y+2 & =\\pm \\,\\frac{4}{3}(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302218 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3be1af41-0c7e-535a-9a86-a5c82122dcdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300250} {:answer/id #uuid "89b9efd6-ce79-56d0-af07-0e42c41ffec0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300659} {:answer/id #uuid "98709d57-b5d1-505c-a13b-2435f487b4d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300728} {:answer/id #uuid "b5fc1198-c5b8-53d4-8b4a-c8b8adf38ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300866} {:answer/id #uuid "b65d5146-dbe1-5221-b3af-8171d063039f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98709d57-b5d1-505c-a13b-2435f487b4d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300728} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ff87f9e-a211-5118-b7af-25e270840e47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301437}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "74c73bef-8f9e-5d05-b33f-448bad8fb053" :question/math-academy-id "q-32570" :question/problem "Consider the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ and the vector $b,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{U},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $⟨{x}_{1},{x}_{2}⟩$ and $⟨{y}_{1},{y}_{2}⟩$ are the solutions to the systems $Ax=b$ and $Ly=b$ respectively, what is the value of ${y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,\\underbrace{(LU)}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL\\underbrace{(Ux)}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=b=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=-4 \\\\ 2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=-6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=-4$ and ${y}_{2}=2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\ny=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $Ux=y.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=y=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2} &=-4 \\\\ -3{x}_{2} &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and ${x}_{1}=2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=-4+3(-\\frac{2}{3})=-6.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302224 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96c4ff95-8613-51d7-a9ef-620e6df2477a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96c4ff95-8613-51d7-a9ef-620e6df2477a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300721} :answer-field/id #uuid "396970b5-d059-558a-a8ad-06f08f053d94" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301414} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "467165d3-c0af-5b88-a79d-84681c3e417d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "467165d3-c0af-5b88-a79d-84681c3e417d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300315} :answer-field/id #uuid "e202a75c-4286-5ff8-b2b1-fdcb40fd80d4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301939}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1bf30272-c0b4-5eee-b418-28d5533437be" :question/math-academy-id "q-325846" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{3}{4},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, then\n\n| $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} $\\sqrt{2}\\,-\\sqrt{14}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive, we take\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{4})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})+(-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{8}-\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{14}}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302226 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a29cdfe-93c9-5408-97f1-ddcbaf3cf83d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a29cdfe-93c9-5408-97f1-ddcbaf3cf83d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300497} :answer-field/id #uuid "3377c8bd-b97b-57ab-a29b-7a5c6e961b59" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301390} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "114f964a-494a-5484-ae82-f1b20326c0a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "114f964a-494a-5484-ae82-f1b20326c0a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300031} :answer-field/id #uuid "e13b9e37-4b86-530f-96dd-7ca308418ec5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dbcc472f-5ffe-5c12-b68c-6360fd3ce661" :question/math-academy-id "q-325854" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, then\n\n| $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} $\\sqrt{2}\\,+\\sqrt{10}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive, we take\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{3})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-(-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{10}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302251 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68579839-b946-5535-9f18-159ca2a0d5bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68579839-b946-5535-9f18-159ca2a0d5bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300486} :answer-field/id #uuid "610e23b8-d11d-5ef6-8730-322a81cb18c4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301539} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c61ec04-038a-51f0-9b16-a0ec69da59c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c61ec04-038a-51f0-9b16-a0ec69da59c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300177} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dbabfdb-ca09-5062-aa79-3848b6b197e0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "74967a64-b648-5512-8822-bc2f06be078f" :question/math-academy-id "q-325991" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=-\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the third quadrant, then\n\n| $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=$ | $=$ | $-\\sqrt{5}\\,-\\,$ {{field-1}} $\\sqrt{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,$ $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡v=1-{sin}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}}{{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{5}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9}-\\frac{5}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{4}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the third quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})(-\\frac{2}{3})-(-\\frac{1}{3})(-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{9}-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\sqrt{5}-4\\sqrt{2}}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302252 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "895ad219-17a6-5bff-8059-11feef8a1657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "895ad219-17a6-5bff-8059-11feef8a1657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300655} :answer-field/id #uuid "094faf34-e8e8-5bb3-b4fb-b094c9773440" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301251} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4705ab53-4430-532d-b491-423a1ff4313a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4705ab53-4430-532d-b491-423a1ff4313a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300321} :answer-field/id #uuid "407d99c3-8188-56b5-a90e-dddc6ae6ea95" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4a1bfb70-9351-599e-8d42-359c4bb40742" :question/math-academy-id "q-325997" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{7}{25},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{24}{25},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=-\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{13}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the fourth quadrant, then\n\n| $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} $\\sqrt{159}\\,-\\,$ {{field-2}} $\\sqrt{10}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $325$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,$ $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡v=1-{sin}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{13})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{10})^{2}}{{13}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{10}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{169}{169}-\\frac{10}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{159}{169}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{159}{169}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{159}}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe’re given that $v$ lies in the fourth quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{159}}{13}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{7}{25})(\\frac{\\sqrt{159}}{13})+(\\frac{24}{25})(-\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{13}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\sqrt{159}}{325}-\\frac{24\\sqrt{10}}{325} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\sqrt{159}-24\\sqrt{10}}{325}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302266 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4374f2ca-d187-5c74-a8e5-694af7f1a10f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300294} {:answer/id #uuid "4a359ac0-f626-5fc9-b0c1-01e620a91911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300341} {:answer/id #uuid "7a50d87f-d58a-5c34-a7a5-6614e8291acf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300564} {:answer/id #uuid "c3a1b26d-8ffa-5c97-9580-8122a3e8e4fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300940} {:answer/id #uuid "d92efac6-01ae-5b2e-8354-9a9f798d247d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a359ac0-f626-5fc9-b0c1-01e620a91911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300341} :answer-field/id #uuid "62bd16e6-f4e0-5d3c-b02e-ace76daef0c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301543}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "468d9e20-789c-5294-9d35-e95d404e4f0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-32617" :question/problem "Given that \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ 7 & 20\\end{bmatrix}$ \nhas the LU decomposition $B=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $B$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $B$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ 7 & 20\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{7}{3}){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot B \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}B=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}B & =U \\\\ B & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ B & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ B & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=\\frac{7}{3},{u}_{12}=9,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=21.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302274 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "503204c2-5202-5eb6-9c99-1d89a4256bc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "503204c2-5202-5eb6-9c99-1d89a4256bc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300382} :answer-field/id #uuid "775ffea0-6f09-507b-ba4a-f5929a9c5ece" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301596} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20d2ae08-199c-5474-adf2-cfd379ea5346" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20d2ae08-199c-5474-adf2-cfd379ea5346" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300115} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fdb03f8-a8af-5dff-897a-1a48b028739f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301740} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8236cfbf-50c1-52a1-9051-6e2573a692b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8236cfbf-50c1-52a1-9051-6e2573a692b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300611} :answer-field/id #uuid "be04d349-7486-598b-9dcd-fc83d123cadf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cefa32c2-7378-5ecb-a3a0-9b87e829889e" :question/math-academy-id "q-326255" :question/problem "Consider the curve with parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi t}{4}),\\,y=sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi t}{6}),\\,0\\le t\\le 5.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | {{field-1}} | $-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |" :question/worked-solution "- To find the value of $x(2),$ we substitute $t=2$ into $x=cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi t}{4}).$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(2)=cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi (2)}{4})=cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $y(3),$ we substitute $t=3$ into $y=sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi t}{6}).$ This gives\n\n$$\ny(3)=sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi (3)}{6})=sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $y(4),$ we substitute $t=4$ into $y=sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi t}{6}).$ This gives\n\n$$\ny(1)=sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi (4)}{6})=sin\\,(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nThen the table is \n\n \n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302275 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cda53e7-8da8-5d86-a98c-5728719bc41b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cda53e7-8da8-5d86-a98c-5728719bc41b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300000} :answer-field/id #uuid "926f273e-d8b0-5e4d-9631-40e611c24ed3" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301693} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25f796a4-f9bc-52c2-95c4-861c28462e0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25f796a4-f9bc-52c2-95c4-861c28462e0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300143} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4c15bfd-ddd4-56df-9248-59fef4cfb8ff" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301912} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "011fae4e-c40e-5543-bf07-c0c1656584a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "011fae4e-c40e-5543-bf07-c0c1656584a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299934} :answer-field/id #uuid "da7a0f49-5d74-598d-9cc7-a44f9d26dfa4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e9040c3c-4511-5ef7-a719-36ef3ae85af2" :question/math-academy-id "q-326256" :question/problem "Consider the curve with parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}),\\,y=tan\\,(\\frac{\\pi t}{3}),\\,-1\\le t\\le 4.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | {{field-1}} | $0$ | {{field-2}} | $0$ | $1$ |\n| $y$ | $-\\sqrt{3}$ | $0$ | {{field-3}} | $-\\sqrt{3}$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ |" :question/worked-solution "- To find the value of $x(0),$ we substitute $t=0$ into $x=cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}).$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(0)=cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi (0)}{2})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $y(1),$ we substitute $t=1$ into $y=tan\\,(\\frac{\\pi t}{3}).$ This gives\n\n$$\ny(1)=tan\\,(\\frac{\\pi (1)}{3})=tan\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $x(2),$ we substitute $t=2$ into $x=cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}).$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(2)=cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi (2)}{2})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=-1.\n$$\n\nThen the table is \n\n \n\n| **$t$** | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| $y$ | $-\\sqrt{3}$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $-\\sqrt{3}$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ |"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302291 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "642793a6-a34a-56ef-9ac0-66acfab64dec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{13}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "642793a6-a34a-56ef-9ac0-66acfab64dec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{13}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300469} :answer-field/id #uuid "758cf272-ca60-5740-8291-4894048c7c1c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b1dc201-81d1-5231-9601-4ced8e8e4925" :question/math-academy-id "q-326363" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{8}{17},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{15}{17},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nIf $v$ lies in the first quadrant, then $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡v=1-{sin}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{9}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{8}{17})(\\frac{4}{5})-(\\frac{15}{17})(\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{85}-\\frac{45}{85} \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{85}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302292 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07fdf48d-ac6b-5ec1-8d05-3fb9d5592051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07fdf48d-ac6b-5ec1-8d05-3fb9d5592051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299969} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dd0e826-e817-5cd1-a6c9-b0bf566ad656" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301267} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "950be932-80bf-5689-a4f2-453b0e731ca8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "950be932-80bf-5689-a4f2-453b0e731ca8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300710} :answer-field/id #uuid "a284af3a-e3f6-539a-9766-5fafad5aeb16" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6128daa9-a33c-5d00-8017-59d8aba58347" :question/math-academy-id "q-326378" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{7}{25},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{24}{25},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the second quadrant, then\n\n| $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)$ | $=$ | {{field-1}}$\\,\\,-\\,$ {{field-2}}$\\sqrt{3}$ | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $50$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the second quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{7}{25})(-\\frac{1}{2})-(\\frac{24}{25})(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{50}-\\frac{24\\sqrt{3}}{50} \\\\  & =\\frac{-7-24\\sqrt{3}}{50}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302302 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c8d2bf8-6141-514a-a1b6-630ff1294a79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "210" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c8d2bf8-6141-514a-a1b6-630ff1294a79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "210" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300178} :answer-field/id #uuid "76ff4139-cb39-5672-853d-64e21d6c8eae" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301594} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "099616f8-60a4-55de-a994-c3adc63c9588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299982}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "099616f8-60a4-55de-a994-c3adc63c9588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299982} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9e6322a-1fd1-5bc5-8adb-141b195549d4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e6e34e6-b66f-59db-bc45-9af5966ce41c" :question/math-academy-id "q-326456" :question/problem "Find the two solutions to the equation $3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$ \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$ \n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-\\sqrt{3} & =2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})={30}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${30}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={30}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={180}^{∘}+{30}^{∘}={210}^{∘}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x={30}^{∘}$ and $x={210}^{∘}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302307 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32440ad6-7f7b-5196-b1ab-98d39e61a9b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300212} {:answer/id #uuid "55772350-0e9f-5ed8-86b3-1bf60cf29e81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300404} {:answer/id #uuid "5c1ccf3a-f7dd-532a-adbc-dd756f877bca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300437} {:answer/id #uuid "aa9a36ce-5c2d-5925-b668-8d23e32923ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300817} {:answer/id #uuid "ee149e2e-60ee-50ed-aa3f-5a5cc9613c68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55772350-0e9f-5ed8-86b3-1bf60cf29e81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300404} :answer-field/id #uuid "3aab5f86-6a49-557c-8f61-783f431a5db8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301416}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b3cc0c68-3553-59ec-9740-0e51c6886431" :question/math-academy-id "q-32662" :question/problem "If $x=y\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+2y,$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at the point $(8,4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3/2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3/2})-\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2})+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{1/2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2y+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{y}-y+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{y}-y+2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(8,4),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(8,4)} & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{4}-4+2)}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302313 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cef3621-5613-513a-9ad2-ffc03e43c43c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300002} {:answer/id #uuid "5bfd641e-3cf0-5617-9544-a0f533f5a594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300436} {:answer/id #uuid "985e6095-ccee-5477-87cb-a76744f1ba5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{83}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300727} {:answer/id #uuid "ed6f600f-2b91-50be-b4b5-c60afa2e0489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "83" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301135} {:answer/id #uuid "faa1af03-8de5-5be0-9ed5-7a51ee805bbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{43}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "985e6095-ccee-5477-87cb-a76744f1ba5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{83}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300727} :answer-field/id #uuid "83bae099-06f3-570f-b2ec-8dfaf4c3aae4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301643}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8588e3b0-fa4a-5436-801f-7b47fb73e332" :question/math-academy-id "q-32706" :question/problem "If $x={y}^{5}+3y-5,$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at the point $(33,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{5}+3y-5) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{5})+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y)-5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(1) \\\\  & =5{y}^{4}+3(1)+0 \\\\  & =5{y}^{4}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{5{y}^{4}+3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(33,2),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(33,2)} & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot {2}^{4}+3}=\\frac{1}{83}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302329 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08da2c73-b3f3-5f9e-bdca-1b5b280e8ca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299976} {:answer/id #uuid "19ab99ee-abb1-5365-b563-f8e7f4d626a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300075} {:answer/id #uuid "19c8433d-ce31-55b1-ace0-59e14900aabb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300076} {:answer/id #uuid "7190b1cb-7787-5bdb-9600-71dbc81662e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300527} {:answer/id #uuid "c9f2b7f6-5083-5e02-a198-e7a6dbb7f6c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19ab99ee-abb1-5365-b563-f8e7f4d626a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300075} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a4a2fa1-bb43-517d-8181-7f72736b0a79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b1cd1efa-f609-59d5-a15c-c2a561c4fae4" :question/math-academy-id "q-32722" :question/problem "Given that\n$K=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nhas the LU decomposition $K=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $K$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $K$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{7}{4}){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot K \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot K \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}K=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}K & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}K & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ K & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ K & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ K & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ K & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302330 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9bdc1b2-8ea1-5127-89c0-6b997d1343aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300968}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9bdc1b2-8ea1-5127-89c0-6b997d1343aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300968} :answer-field/id #uuid "99c7c1d5-e064-5947-ad92-edb4b63aee4f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5fc8ff6e-fc33-5efd-ab02-f17a877d633b" :question/math-academy-id "q-327247" :question/problem "Find the exact value of ${(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}))}^{2}.$\n\n${(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}))}^{2}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses and use the Pythagorean identity.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}))}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =[{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})]+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's recall the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=1+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}))}^{2}=1+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302332 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d53bf1bf-3240-597c-8ba6-91c426822271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d53bf1bf-3240-597c-8ba6-91c426822271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301022} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d2fa77f-5bcb-5df5-8444-35c66cd13a26" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301370}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c456891a-3eac-5005-ae95-48c26bfb98c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-327277" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{8})}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{8})}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{8})}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{8})\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{8})}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{8})\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{8})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{8})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302334 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f75d8f5-95a0-575d-bf5b-8a950225640c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300022} {:answer/id #uuid "1b8dae1a-4161-5391-bb7e-3c8f740a33ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300089} {:answer/id #uuid "389608b6-9f5e-5b60-abd7-98607139d9c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300235} {:answer/id #uuid "4ebe4f92-863a-5315-b6cb-2db53229498e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300373} {:answer/id #uuid "cf58a871-09ba-503d-af1f-bc8780ad8fd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300996}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "389608b6-9f5e-5b60-abd7-98607139d9c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300235} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fbc3d08-c28b-5507-a81c-958a63ad9f3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0bc11750-a747-519f-92bc-e4276b877d58" :question/math-academy-id "q-32742" :question/problem "$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIf $C=LU$ is the LU decomposition of the matrix $C$ given above, what is the value of ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{22}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply our standard LU factorization algorithm.\n\nWe reduce the matrix $C$ to row echelon form to obtain the matrix $U,$ as usual. However, instead of writing zeros below the main diagonal, we will instead write down the entries of the matrix $L.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -6 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 3 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -6 & -7 \\\\ \\,\\,0\\,\\, & -\\frac{1}{2} & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nL=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,U=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${l}_{32}=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${u}_{22}=-6,$ we get\n\n$$\n{l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{22}=(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (-6)=3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302335 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e744601-b0f0-5c80-90b1-52f424880be7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300010} {:answer/id #uuid "3356f367-46a8-5259-baf4-8992f15adc4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300217} {:answer/id #uuid "33e7c7a9-e193-5761-8a7d-e1615296fe7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300220} {:answer/id #uuid "55d34e98-fabd-5bc8-815f-0ec9c01d9998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300408} {:answer/id #uuid "a4a3d08a-fc50-5895-88e3-57cafeb804fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300792}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55d34e98-fabd-5bc8-815f-0ec9c01d9998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300408} :answer-field/id #uuid "13417974-b035-52d3-865d-f3ffcc9608f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "27eaf5c2-ea09-500f-97aa-b8eb299f8da3" :question/math-academy-id "q-32749" :question/problem "$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ -9 & -6 & -6 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIf $D=LU$ is the LU decomposition of the matrix $D$ given above, what is the value of ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{23}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply our standard LU factorization algorithm.\n\nWe reduce the matrix $D$ to row echelon form to obtain the matrix $U,$ as usual. However, instead of writing zeros below the main diagonal, we will instead write down the entries of the matrix $L.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ -9 & -6 & -6 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 9 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+1{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 9 \\\\ -1 & \\,\\frac{1}{3}\\, & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nL=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & \\frac{1}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,U=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${l}_{32}=\\frac{1}{3}$ and ${u}_{23}=9,$ we get\n\n$$\n{l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{23}=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 9=3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302343 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb9cb646-12e0-5cc9-a5a0-d97db7f6720b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300979}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb9cb646-12e0-5cc9-a5a0-d97db7f6720b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300979} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffb945c6-18c8-5c3e-8121-d1b3da73260f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e82816cb-fa9a-5239-a243-6d4c247f2e0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-327714" :question/problem "Given that the expression $\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$\n\n$ϕ=$ \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=\\sqrt{6} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ ϕ & =arctan\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\ ϕ & =30\\,{\\,}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]
